{"name": "recency_forgetting_concepts", "outcome": "rejected", "detail": "too slow to fit: the fit of 'recency_forgetting_concepts' (target_accept 0.99) was still sampling after the 30-minute limit and was stopped. Every sampling run of a candidate's admission fit has a 30-minute limit. Make the model cheaper to evaluate and easier to sample: vectorise the likelihood over trials (no Python loops, pytensor scan or per-trial subgraphs), compute features once per unique sequence (in compute_features or prepare_observed, not in the graph), and drop or merge parameters the data barely constrain — a weakly identified posterior makes NUTS take maximal-length trajectories.", "hypothesis": "People learn the program's concept by conservative Bayesian concept learning over arithmetic rules and numeric intervals (size principle), but they reason only from the examples they still hold in memory when the target is asked: the last example shown is always remembered, while earlier examples drop out of memory with a probability that grows the further back in the sequence they were shown. A forgotten example neither rules out concepts nor sharpens them, so generalization is driven mostly by the most recent examples, and reordering the same examples changes which targets get a \"yes\" (targets that fit only the first example are endorsed less often than ones fitting only the last).", "context": "experiment1 round 2 candidate 0 lens 6"}
{"name": "digit_place_similarity_gradient", "outcome": "admitted", "detail": "", "hypothesis": "People judge a target by its similarity to the examples in their written decimal form rather than on the number line: they compare the target with each example place by place, and every mismatch in the units digit or in the tens digit multiplies similarity down by its own inferred factor. The more similar the target is on average to the examples under this digit-place metric, the more likely people are to say \"yes\" — so 21 after 91, or 58 after 28 and 98, is endorsed despite being numerically far away, while 88 after 89 is rejected despite being adjacent.", "context": "experiment1 round 2 candidate 1 lens 7"}
{"name": "soft_criterion_concept_decision", "outcome": "admitted", "detail": "", "hypothesis": "People infer the hidden concept by plain Bayesian concept learning over arithmetic rules and numeric intervals (the simplest current inference, with the size principle), but they do not report that belief by probability matching: they answer \"yes\" when their confidence that the target belongs clears a personal criterion, applied noisily. How high the criterion sits (a bias toward \"yes\" or \"no\") and how sharply confidence is turned into a choice are the only changes, so moderately believed targets are pushed toward one answer and near-certain beliefs are softened toward the other.", "context": "experiment1 round 2 candidate 2 lens 8"}
{"name": "session_drifting_evidence_concepts", "outcome": "rejected", "detail": "too slow to fit: the fit of 'session_drifting_evidence_concepts' (target_accept 0.99) was still sampling after the 30-minute limit and was stopped. Every sampling run of a candidate's admission fit has a 30-minute limit. Make the model cheaper to evaluate and easier to sample: vectorise the likelihood over trials (no Python loops, pytensor scan or per-trial subgraphs), compute features once per unique sequence (in compute_features or prepare_observed, not in the graph), and drop or merge parameters the data barely constrain — a weakly identified posterior makes NUTS take maximal-length trajectories.", "hypothesis": "Refinement of `sample_size_growing_evidence_concepts`: people do the same conservative Bayesian concept learning over the 42 arithmetic rules and midpoint-weighted intervals of 1-100, with evidence weight growing with the number of examples, but how much weight they give the examples' evidence drifts over the session — as the task wears on, people scrutinise the examples less and so hold a flatter, broader posterior over concepts. The one change is that the evidence strength is scaled by an exponential drift in the trial's position in the session (rate inferred), which predicts more liberal, broader generalization (more \"yes\") late in the session, the discrepancy the incumbent's critique flags.", "context": "experiment1 round 2 candidate 3 refine incumbent sample_size_growing_evidence_concepts"}
{"name": "session_drift_liberal_concepts", "outcome": "rejected", "detail": "model binds ['trial_index'] — response-row bookkeeping a bare stimulus never carries — so it cannot be evaluated on a stimulus pool (the novelty gate now, the held-out evaluation later); compute everything from number_set and target.", "hypothesis": "Refinement of `sample_size_growing_evidence_concepts`: people do the same conservative Bayesian concept learning over the 42 arithmetic rules and midpoint-weighted intervals of 1-100, with evidence weight growing with set size, but as the session wears on they grow more liberal, increasingly accepting targets their concept does not contain. The one change is a \"yes\"-drift: with a probability that changes smoothly (logistically, slope inferred) with how far into the session the trial is, people answer \"yes\" regardless of the concept, which predicts the rising yes-rate across the session that the incumbent's critique flags (and reduces to a constant yes-bias if the slope is zero).", "context": "experiment1 round 2 candidate 4 refine incumbent sample_size_growing_evidence_concepts"}
{"name": "first_example_weak_erlang_concepts", "outcome": "admitted", "detail": "", "hypothesis": "Refinement of `first_example_weak_sampling_concepts`: people update their concept one example at a time, and the first example only rules out concepts that do not contain it, while every later example is treated as drawn from the concept (size principle, weighted by an inferred strength) — but people also hold a prior preference for number ranges of intermediate size (Tenenbaum's Erlang size prior on intervals, scale inferred, grafted from `iter1_candidate4`). The one change is this size prior: because a single example carries no size-principle evidence under this account, what people generalize to from one example is set entirely by their prior, and the size prior predicts a moderately wide range around a lone example rather than the broad ranges near 1-100 that a size-blind prior favours.", "context": "experiment1 round 2 candidate 5 refine chosen"}
{"name": "recency_decayed_evidence_concepts", "outcome": "admitted", "detail": "", "hypothesis": "People learn the program's concept by conservative Bayesian concept learning over arithmetic rules and numeric intervals (size principle), but their memory of the examples fades with how long ago each was shown: the last example counts as full evidence, and each example further back in the sequence counts as progressively weaker evidence, by a constant inferred retention factor per position. Generalization is therefore driven mostly by the most recent examples, so reordering the same examples changes which targets get a \"yes\" (targets that fit only the first example are endorsed less often than targets that fit only the last).", "context": "experiment1 round 2 candidate 0 lens 6 repair 1"}
{"name": "session_drifting_yes_bias_concepts", "outcome": "rejected", "detail": "model binds ['trial_index'] — response-row bookkeeping a bare stimulus never carries — so it cannot be evaluated on a stimulus pool (the novelty gate now, the held-out evaluation later); compute everything from number_set and target.", "hypothesis": "Refinement of `sample_size_growing_evidence_concepts`: people do the same conservative Bayesian concept learning over the 42 arithmetic rules and midpoint-weighted intervals of 1-100, with evidence weight growing with the number of examples, but their willingness to say \"yes\" given that belief drifts over the session — as the task wears on, people become more liberal and endorse targets they are only partly confident about. The one change is a response bias on the log-odds of the posterior belief that shifts linearly with the trial's position in the session (rate inferred, zero at mid-session), which predicts a higher yes-rate late in the session than early, the discrepancy the incumbent's critique flags.", "context": "experiment1 round 2 candidate 3 refine incumbent sample_size_growing_evidence_concepts repair 1"}
{"name": "lapsing_growing_evidence_concepts", "outcome": "admitted", "detail": "", "hypothesis": "Refinement of `sample_size_growing_evidence_concepts`: people do the same conservative Bayesian concept learning over the 42 arithmetic rules and midpoint-weighted intervals of 1-100, with evidence weight growing with set size, but on some trials they answer without consulting their concept at all, guessing \"yes\" or \"no\" with their own inferred rates. The one change is this attention-lapse response floor and ceiling (an inferred chance of a blind \"yes\" and a separate chance of a blind \"no\"), which predicts a steady baseline of \"yes\" to targets the concept clearly excludes and occasional \"no\" to targets it clearly contains, where the incumbent puts both at near-certainty.", "context": "experiment1 round 2 candidate 4 refine incumbent sample_size_growing_evidence_concepts repair 1"}
{"name": "decade_concept_bayesian_learning", "outcome": "admitted", "detail": "", "hypothesis": "People do the same conservative Bayesian concept learning as the best model (42 arithmetic rules and midpoint-weighted intervals of 1-100, evidence weight growing with set size, attention lapses), but they also hold decades — \"numbers in the 20s\", \"numbers in the 60s\" (same tens digit) — as named concepts with their own inferred prior share, split equally across the ten decades. Because a decade is a privileged, a-priori-likely range rather than one of thousands of arbitrary intervals, a target just outside the examples' range is endorsed more when it shares their tens decade than when an equally close target crosses a decade boundary.", "context": "experiment1 round 3 candidate 0 lens 9"}
{"name": "coherence_attention_exemplar_similarity", "outcome": "admitted", "detail": "", "hypothesis": "People do not infer a concept; they judge the target by its similarity to the stored examples (exemplar generalization), but their attention is drawn to the dimensions on which the stored examples cohere: every property all the examples share (parity, final digit, decade, contained digit, prime, square, cube, power-of-k, multiple-of-k) gets attention in proportion to how distinctive it is in 1-100, so a target that lacks a rare shared property (e.g. \"ends in 8\", \"in the 20s\") is judged dissimilar to every example, while closeness on the number line counts on a scale that stretches with the examples' spread. Saying \"yes\" is a Luce choice between the target's mean similarity to the examples and a fixed criterion.", "context": "experiment1 round 3 candidate 1 lens 10"}
{"name": "transformational_distance_similarity", "outcome": "admitted", "detail": "", "hypothesis": "People do not induce a concept or measure closeness on the number line; they judge whether the target can be made from one of the examples by a short chain of simple mental operations on numbers (add or subtract one, add or subtract ten, double, halve, reverse the digits), so the fewer operations it takes to turn the examples into the target, the more likely they say \"yes\" (transformational similarity). This predicts \"yes\" to 32 after 16, to 61 after 16, and to 40 after 20 even though they are far away on the number line, while a numerically moderate jump that needs many steps (e.g. 47 after 20) is rejected.", "context": "experiment1 round 3 candidate 2 lens 0"}
{"name": "decade_aligned_interval_concepts", "outcome": "admitted", "detail": "", "hypothesis": "Refinement of `lapsing_growing_evidence_concepts`: people do the same conservative Bayesian concept learning over the 42 arithmetic rules and midpoint-weighted intervals of 1-100, with evidence weight growing with set size and attention lapses, but they mentally carve the number line into decades, so number ranges whose edges fall on decade boundaries (e.g. 20-29, 30-59, 1-10) are a priori more plausible than arbitrary ranges by an inferred factor. The one change is this decade-aligned prior bonus on intervals, which predicts that a nearby target outside the examples' span is endorsed more often when it shares a decade with an example (25 after 21, 23) than when it lies just across a decade boundary (31 after 27, 29) — the same-decade bonus the incumbent's critique flags.", "context": "experiment1 round 3 candidate 3 refine incumbent lapsing_growing_evidence_concepts"}
